                     unsafe JS Numbers are rejected, never guessed"}}
 :kotoba.lang.value-codec/code-identity "lang/code-identity.edn"
 :kotoba.lang.value-codec/execution-boundary
 "90-docs/adr/2607252500-kotoba-wasm-component-first-execution-boundary.edn"

 :kotoba.lang.value-codec/scope
 "One canonical encoding for every Kotoba EDN value that acquires an identity
  or crosses a content-addressed boundary. Covers the value position only;
  embedding structures (semantic definition IR, prolly-tree leaf, datom) keep
  their own envelopes and call this codec per value."

 :kotoba.lang.value-codec/invariant
 "Encoding is a total, deterministic function of the value, byte-identical on
  every runtime. An unadmitted type is rejected closed, never widened
  silently and never re-tagged as a neighbouring type."

 :encoding
 {:form "dag-cbor 2-element array [type-code payload]"
  :rationale
  "DAG-CBOR has no keyword, symbol, or set type, so a direct mapping cannot
   distinguish :a from \"a\"; a single-key escape map would collide with a
   user map carrying that key. A uniform tag removes both ambiguities."
  :overhead "~2 bytes per scalar"
  :code-stability
  "codes are wire constants: append only, never renumber a landed code —
   a renumber silently reinterprets stored bytes"}

 :admitted
 {:nil     {:code 0  :payload :cbor/null}
  :boolean {:code 1  :payload :cbor/bool}
  :integer {:code 2  :payload :cbor/int
            :bounds {:min -9007199254740991 :max 9007199254740991}
            :bounds-reason
            "cbor.core/byte-at documents that its cljs path is exact only to
             2^53 (JS bitwise operators truncate to Int32 first, so it divides
             instead). A wider integer would silently produce different bytes
             on the two runtimes."
            :wide-integer-rule "exact signed i64 uses the explicit code-9
                                wrapper; bare integers never widen silently"}
  :float   {:code 3  :payload :cbor/byte-string
            :payload-form "8 bytes, big-endian IEEE-754 binary64"
            :requires-wrapper "ipld.value/float64"
            :wrapper-reason
            "JS cannot distinguish 1.0 from 1 — (integer? 1.0) is true on cljs
             and false on the JVM — so classifying a bare number by runtime
             type would give one source program two encodings. A bare
             non-integral number is rejected closed. Same discipline
             ipld.core already applies to Link."
            :not-cbor-float-reason
            "cbor.core implements a deliberately tight profile with no float
             support; adding major-type-7 floats would change a dependency
             every content-addressed repo in the org already pins. The codec's
             own tag envelope makes CBOR-native float typing unnecessary."
            :rejects [:nan :positive-infinity :negative-infinity :negative-zero]
            :negative-zero-note
            "-0.0 is rejected rather than normalized, so the boundary is
             visible at authoring time instead of silently changing identity"
            :f32-note ":f32 stays a binder-level type annotation
                       (semantic-type-block), not a wire type"}
  :string  {:code 4  :payload :cbor/text
            :requires "valid UTF-8, no lone surrogates"}
  :keyword {:code 5  :payload :cbor/text :form "ns/name | name"
            :requires "the text must read back as the identical value"}
  :symbol  {:code 6  :payload :cbor/text :form "ns/name | name"
            :requires "the text must read back as the identical value"}
  :bytes   {:code 7  :payload :cbor/byte-string}
  :link    {:code 8  :payload :cbor/tag-42 :type "ipld.link/Link"
            :compatibility-api "ipld.core/link"}
  :int64   {:code 9 :payload :cbor/byte-string
            :payload-form "8 bytes, big-endian signed two's-complement"
            :requires-wrapper "kotoba.value.codec/int64"
            :bounds {:min -9223372036854775808
                     :max 9223372036854775807}
            :cljs-input :javascript-bigint
            :unsafe-js-number :rejected}
  :vector  {:code 16 :payload :cbor/array :order :source}
  :list    {:code 17 :payload :cbor/array :order :source
            :note "distinct from vector; both are ordered"}
  :set     {:code 18 :payload :cbor/array :order :encoded-element-bytes-unsigned}
  :map     {:code 19 :payload :cbor/array-of-pairs :order :encoded-key-bytes-unsigned}}

 :rejected-closed
 [:ratio :char :uuid :inst :record :function :var :regex
  :bare-non-integral-number
  :any-tagged-literal :any-cbor-tag-other-than-42
  :indefinite-length-item :bare-integer-outside-safe-range]

 :determinism
 {:definite-length-only true
  :allowed-cbor-tags #{42}
  :canonical-minor-form true
  :sort-key :encoded-bytes-unsigned
  :sort-rule "shorter encoding first, then bytewise unsigned — the same rule
              cbor.core applies to DAG-CBOR map keys"
  :unsigned-reason
  "a raw JVM byte array compared signed orders 0x80-0xff BEFORE 0x00, while a
   cljs Uint8Array orders them after; a sort leaking the platform's signedness
   is byte-divergence hiding inside a 'deterministic' sort"
  :resolved-consumer-drift
  "VC4 replaced kotoba semantic-code's signed JVM byte ordering with this
   codec's unsigned canonical ordering"
  :not-sort-key [:source-order :pr-str-text]
  :cross-runtime ["jvm" "cljs"]
  :evidence-required :shared-conformance-vectors
  :precedent
  "compiler:src/kotoba/compiler/artifact.cljc:7-29 records a live JVM/nbb
   digest divergence caused by pr-str rendering a bigint differently. Vectors
   ship with VC1, not after."
  :vc1-findings
  ["NaN is NOT detectable with (not= d d): clojure.lang.Util.equiv
    short-circuits on reference identity, so two boxed references to the same
    NaN compare equal and the check silently passes. Platform interop states
    the intent directly and cannot be defeated by boxing."]}

 :consumers
 {:io-ipld
  {:repo "kotoba-lang/io-ipld"
   :change "provide kotoba.value.codec as the stable bounded facade over
            ipld.value; isolate ipld.link and dependency-free base32 so
            value-only CLJS consumers do not load CID hashing/npm code"
   :unchanged ["ipld.core/encode" "ipld.core/decode"]
   :reason "encode/decode are a NODE codec: ->cbor-data (src/ipld/core.cljc:93)
            passes keyword map keys through unchanged and decode returns
            strings; set? is neither map? nor sequential? so a set reaches
            cbor/encode with no defined element order."
   :status :implemented}
  :arrangement
  {:repo "kotoba-lang/arrangement"
   :change "index-root leaf VALUE via encode-value; schema-version 1 -> 2"
   :scope :value-slot-only
   :key-path-unchanged true
   :key-path-reason
   "blind-fn is a caller-supplied keyed MAC whose output a cold reader
    re-derives to seek by prefix (kotobase-peer cold-datoms), so changing its
    input is a cross-repo crypto change, not a local one. Typed round-tripping
    is the value slot's job — which is exactly where the type was being lost."
   :key-path-restriction
   {:admits [:nil :boolean :integer :string :keyword :symbol :ipld-link]
    :rejects-with :arrangement/component-not-blindable
    :reason
    "blind-fn serializes what it is handed and the reference implementation
     pr-strs it. pr-str is canonical for only some values: a set/map has
     undefined iteration order, a byte array prints an identity hash (two
     arrays with the SAME bytes blind differently), a record prints
     platform-dependently. None of these were rejected before — they produced
     divergent keys quietly."}
   :link->edn-retained true
   :link->edn-reason
   "NOT obsolete as the vc0 draft assumed: a leaf key is still built with
    pr-str, and pr-str of a Link record is platform-dependent record printing.
    link->edn stays on the key path; edn->link stays as the version-1 read
    path."
   :note "the hot db is already general — assert-quad (core.cljc:44-56) stores
          s/p/o as ordinary map keys. The restriction is only at persistence."
   :status :implemented}
  :semantic-code
  {:repo "kotoba-lang/kotoba"
   :change "normalize-literal delegates every scalar value position to the
            codec; quoted forms go to the codec whole; set/map literal order
            recomputed over unsigned bytes; default-contract-cid names the
            codec"
   :closes "float literals rejected while :f32 is already a semantic metadata
            key and a semantic-type value type"
   :new-dependency
   {:lib "io.github.kotoba-lang/io-ipld"
    :note "kotoba had no io-ipld dependency. Only the VALUE codec is used --
           ipld.core/encode (the node codec) is not. kotoba's cbor and
           multiformats pins deliberately stay NEWER than io-ipld's own (they
           carry the truncated-input and unsigned-key-comparator fixes);
           io-ipld's suite was re-run green against those newer pins before
           the dependency was added."}
   :collection-literals-stay-expressions
   "a vector/set/map LITERAL in code has expression elements ([x (+ 1 2)]), so
    those branches still recurse through normalize-expr. Only quoted forms are
    data. The codec owns the scalar vocabulary and all of quoted data."
   :float-literal-boundary
   "admitted where the reader preserves float-ness (jvm). On cljs a source 1.0
    and a source 1 are the same runtime value, so a non-integral literal is
    rejected rather than guessed: fail-closed, never two encodings for one
    source program. semantic-code's consumers are all .clj today."
   :status :implemented}
  :actor-ipc
  {:repo "kotoba-lang/actor-ipc"
   :merge "3af2442b4bf7ea8dfe8cf2333f17270f1e95573e"
   :change "replace Delta pr-str/read-string pseudo-bytes with a versioned,
            bounded actor-ipc.delta/v1 envelope over kotoba.value.v1"
   :ability-limit-bytes 16777216
   :legacy-edn-text :rejected
   :float-adaptation :private-explicit-float64
   :public-api [:delta-to-bytes :delta-from-bytes]
   :runtime-dependencies :kotoba-lang-org-only
   :verified {:jvm {:tests 7 :assertions 31}
              :cljs-node {:tests 7 :assertions 31}
              :kotoba-targets [:js-browser :wasm32-browser]}
   :syntax-finding "wire tags, Float64 wrappers, canonical ordering and byte
                    limits stay outside authored Kotoba and actor domain data"
   :status :implemented}
  :provider
  {:repo "kotoba-lang/provider"
   :merge "db41ea922687df56e99d32b03b74b51e4cd418c8"
   :boundary :provider.ops-audit/v1
   :change "move all six ops-kit wrapper and W4 round-trip audit paths from
            EDN-only evidence to bounded canonical request/reply value bytes"
   :ability-limit-bytes 1048576
   :legacy-edn-text :compatibility-input
   :canonical-output-fields [:request-value-bytes :reply-value-bytes]
   :decode-validation [:exact-envelope-keys :format :kit :direction]
   :tagged-literals :rejected
   :verified {:jvm {:tests 243 :assertions 2014}
              :cljs-node :real-compiler-roundtrip
              :compiler-consumer {:target :aarch64-kotoba-v1
                                  :passed 16 :total 16}}
   :syntax-finding "provider audit values cross as semantic request/reply bytes;
                    EDN survives only as explicit W4 compatibility evidence"
   :status :implemented}}

 :migration
 {:arrangement
  {:schema-version {:from 1 :to 2}
   :required true
   :reason "restore rejects unknown schema versions, so a v1 snapshot must
            have a declared path before the bump lands"
   :rule "a v1 snapshot either migrates or is rejected with a named reason;
          it is never reinterpreted under the v2 index shape"
   :chosen :read-compatible
   :chosen-detail
   "restore dispatches on the version the snapshot RECORDS: version 1 leaves
    decode through the old node codec + edn->link, version 2 through
    kotoba.value.v1. No rewrite is required and no existing store stops
    opening. Writing is always current-schema-version."
   :supported-read-versions #{1 2}
   :rejects-with :arrangement/unsupported-schema-version
   :honest-boundary
   "a version-1 snapshot cannot retroactively recover a type it never
    persisted: a keyword written under version 1 comes back as the string it
    was stored as. VC3 fixes what is written from now on, it does not
    reinterpret old bytes."
   :cross-repo-landing
   "arrangement pins io-ipld by :git/sha, so landing requires committing
    io-ipld's ipld.value and bumping that pin; verified locally with a
    :local/root override"}
  :semantic-code
  {:lands-on :elaborated-contract-v2
   :site "src/kotoba/semantic_code.cljc:270-273"
   :rule "v1 definition CIDs remain verifiable under the recorded v1 contract;
          a new codec never reinterprets an old CID"}}

 :host-arguments
 {:status :dependent-stage
  :stage :vc5
  :lowering "constant collection literal in host-arg position -> data segment
             of canonical bytes -> (ptr,len)"
  :mirrors "compiler:resources/kotoba/lang/guest-grammar.edn:195 :string-host-arg"
  :unblocks
  ["structured host arguments leave the 127-UTF-8-byte portable-string bound
    (guest-grammar.edn:194)"
   "the argument survives into typed KIR, where normalize-literal already
    yields a definition identity at no added hash-contract cost"
   "the host decodes a bounded typed value instead of parsing an opaque string
    (kotoba:src/kotoba/wasm_exec.clj:294)"]
  :constants-only true
  :constants-only-reason
  "quote is not a core special form and keyword literals lower to FNV-1a
   integer identity, so a guest cannot rebuild an EDN value at runtime"
  :implemented-as
  {:grammar "kotoba-lang/grammar :data-head-host-ops + :sugar :data-host-arg;
             initial data-head set is the kgraph-* family"
   :authority "kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang lang/guest-grammar.edn (vendored into
               consumers, which each carry their own classpath copy)"
   :lowering "kotoba:src/kotoba/runtime.clj lower-language-forms --
              data-host-arg-literal? + the data-head branch of lower-string-head"
   :no-new-machinery
   "memory-layout runs AFTER lower-language-forms and already lays out every
    all-integer vector literal as a data segment, so the lowered form reuses
    the existing bytes-ptr/bytes-len path exactly"
   :host "kotoba:src/kotoba/wasm_exec.clj read-host-value"
   :wire-discriminator
   {:rule "first byte 0x82 -> kotoba.value.v1; otherwise legacy EDN text"
    :exactness
    "not a heuristic: every value encodes as a CBOR 2-element array (head byte
     0x82), and 0x82 is a UTF-8 CONTINUATION byte, so no valid UTF-8 -- and
     therefore no valid EDN text -- can begin with it"}
   :excluded
   {:all-integer-vector
    "already bytes-ptr/bytes-len's raw-byte literal; silently re-encoding it as
     a canonical value would change the bytes an existing guest hands its host"}
   :no-new-call-head
   "the same host op with a differently-shaped first argument, so the
    strict-grammar admission set is unaffected"}}

 :non-goals
 {:cache-key
  {:claim-forbidden "query identity + snapshot identity is effect-free"
   :reason "commit! rebuilds all four prolly trees over every triple on every
            commit (arrangement:src/arrangement/core.cljc:154-160, 202-219) —
            O(N), not O(delta). Query stays an effect excluded from the shared
            cache (kotoba:src/kotoba/shared_semantic_cache.clj:70-72)."}
  :capability-relaxation
  {:claim-forbidden "a query CID authorizes a scoped query"
   :reason "kgraph-query stays denied under every scoped grant
            (kotoba:src/kotoba/wasm_exec.clj:290-296): a query CID does not
            bound which entities a join returns, and a parameterized query
            shares one CID across different result scopes."}
  :replaces-canonical-edn-text
  {:claim-forbidden "one encoding for everything"
   :reason "signed supply-chain objects stay on artifact.cljc canonical EDN
            text because they are meant to be read and reviewed as EDN."}
  :deterministic-aead
  {:claim-forbidden "commit! is content-addressed regardless of crypto fns"
   :reason "the claim is conditional on blind-fn/encrypt-fn determinism
            (arrangement:src/arrangement/core.cljc:145-152); this codec does
            not change that and does not weaken ADR-2607051000."}
  :guest-reader
  {:claim-forbidden "guests may read or construct EDN at runtime"
   :reason "no guest-side reader, tagged-literal extension point, reader macro,
            or runtime eval is introduced."}}

 :safety
 {:closes
  ["a keyword persisted and read back as a string"
   "a set persisted in implementation-defined order"]
  :why-these-matter
  "both currently produce a value different from what was written while
   remaining validly CID-addressed — integrity checks pass on the wrong value"
  :decode-revalidates
  "decode-value re-checks canonical set/map order and rejects duplicates, so a
   peer cannot return a canonically-ADDRESSED block whose contents are not in
   canonical FORM"
  :retains ["kotoba:src/kotoba/bounded_cbor.clj:7-12 admission bounds apply
             before decoding"]
  :grants-nothing "a value CID is not an authority; VC5 adds no permission
                   that VC0 did not already have"}

